hypotheses.statement:
For $j\geq2$, every $$x\in\mathcal N_2^{2,\,4\cdot3^{j+1}}
 \quad\text{with}\quad
 \Phi(x)\in\mathbf F_3^\times b_j$$ has nonzero image in $H^2(G;E_{4\cdot3^{j+1}})$.

The only possible differential between $E_{34}(H)$ and $E_{105}(H)$ is $$\label{h:d53}
 d_{53}(C)=e_{53}A^{-8}C^2U,\qquad d_{53}(U)=0,
 \qquad e_{53}\in k.$$

On transfers, the length-seventeen chain anchored at a scalar with $w_0=3p$ is $$\label{g:d17-exact}
 0\longrightarrow\mathcal T_n
 \xrightarrow{\quad h\quad}\mathcal W_{n-2}
 \xrightarrow{\quad h\wedge\quad}\mathcal T_{n-4}
 \longrightarrow0,$$ with its arrows scaled by $p$ and $p+1$, respectively, up to nonzero units.

There is a triangle $$\label{cube:T-triangle}
 \Sigma^3M\xrightarrow{g}M\xrightarrow{j_T}T
      \xrightarrow{\pi_T}\Sigma^4M$$ and a map $F:\Sigma^NM\to T$ such that $$\pi_TF=x^3 1_M.$$

For $k\geq1$, multiplication by $b^k$ into $D_{f,d}$ is surjective if $$\label{finite:surjective-bound}
 d<S_k(f):=\frac92f+\frac12-6k,$$ and injective if $$\label{finite:injective-bound}
 d<I_k(f):=\frac92f-4-6k=S_k(f-1).$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Fix $j\geq0$. For $$n\in\{j,j-2,\ldots,j\bmod2\},\qquad
 d=3^n,\qquad L=\frac{3^{j+1-n}+1}{4},$$ the classes $$\label{det:uncorrected-beta}
 \beta'_{Ld/d}=\delta_3\delta_{v_1^d}(v_2^{Ld})$$ form an $\mathbf F_3$-basis of $\mathcal N_2^{2,\,4\cdot3^{j+1}}$. Every member with $L>1$ has zero Thom image.

For $j\geq2$, every $$x\in\mathcal N_2^{2,\,4\cdot3^{j+1}}
 \quad\text{with}\quad
 \Phi(x)\in\mathbf F_3^\times b_j$$ has nonzero image in $H^2(G;E_{4\cdot3^{j+1}})$.

Let $j\geq2$, and let $\alpha\in\pi_{4\cdot3^{j+1}-2}S_{(3)}$ be detected by $b_j$ in the mod-$3$ Adams spectral sequence. Its image under the unit $$\pi_{4\cdot3^{j+1}-2}S_{(3)}
 \longrightarrow
 \pi_{4\cdot3^{j+1}-2}(E^{hG})$$ is nonzero and has finite additive order. Its filtration-two symbol is the nonzero image of the Adams–Novikov representative in Lemma <a href="#det:filtration-comparison" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="det:filtration-comparison">4</a>.

Set $x_i=g^i x_0$, with indices read modulo nine. Then $$\label{coef:ring-model}
 \left(
 \frac{W(k)[x_0,\ldots,x_8,(x_0\cdots x_8)^{-1}]}
 {(x_i+x_{i+3}+x_{i+6}:0\le i<3)}
 \right)^{\wedge}_{(3,x_i/x_0-1)}
 \xrightarrow{\ \cong\ } E_*$$ is an isomorphism of $W(k)$-linear graded $G$-rings, with $|x_i|=-2$ and $g(x_i)=x_{i+1}$.

One has $\mu\in\mathbf F_3^{\times}$; in integer exponents we use its representative $1$ or $-1$. Moreover, $$\Psi=\mu\Theta\wedge(-)$$ on the complementary two-plane, and $$\begin{aligned}
 \label{h:e6}
 E_6(H)&=K[A^{\pm1},B^{\pm1}]
       \otimes\Lambda\bigl(\Theta,b^{-1-\mu}(V/K\theta)\bigr),\\
 \label{h:e10}
 E_{10}(H)&=S_0[A^{\pm1},B^{\pm1}]
       \otimes\Lambda\bigl(b^{-1-\mu}W\bigr).

\end{aligned}$$ The $d_9$ is the Koszul differential on $\Theta,F$ and is zero on the other displayed generators.

One has $\epsilon\ne0$ and $\eta/\epsilon=2$. After rescaling $h$, the $d_{17}$ coefficients in <a href="#h:first-plane-coefficients" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:first-plane-coefficients">[h:first-plane-coefficients]</a> are $p$ and $p-1$. The next nonzero change is $d_{33}$, which contracts the surviving $\ell$-generator by a unit times $Z$. Its cohomology is $$\label{h:e34}
 E_{34}(H)=k[A^{\pm1},C^{\pm1}]\otimes\Lambda(U),
 \qquad C=B^3=b^9,\qquad U=BX.$$

The only possible differential between $E_{34}(H)$ and $E_{105}(H)$ is $$\label{h:d53}
 d_{53}(C)=e_{53}A^{-8}C^2U,\qquad d_{53}(U)=0,
 \qquad e_{53}\in k.$$ The class $A^{18}C^{-2}U$ survives to $E_{105}(H)$, and $$\label{h:d105}
 d_{105}(A^{18}C^{-2}U)\in k^{\times}.$$ In particular, $E_{106}(H)=0$.

At fixed $r_0,n$, the $d_5$ complex in increasing exterior degree is $$\label{g:d5-complex}
 \widetilde P_n\xrightarrow{\ r_0\operatorname{res}\ }P_n
 \xrightarrow{\ \pm(r_0+1)D\ }P_n
 \xrightarrow{\ (r_0+2)\operatorname{tr}\ }\widetilde P_n.$$ Consequently the $E_6$ page has the following rows: $$\label{g:E6}
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
 r_0&j=0&j=1&j=2&j=3\\\hline
 0&\widetilde P_n&P_n^Q&0&\overline k_n\\
 2&\overline k_n&P_n/P_n^Q&DP_n&\overline k_n\\
 1&\overline k_n&0&(P_n)_Q&\widetilde P_n.
\end{array}$$ The orientation $u'$ has zero $d_5$ and $d_9$.

On transfers, the length-seventeen chain anchored at a scalar with $w_0=3p$ is $$\label{g:d17-exact}
 0\longrightarrow\mathcal T_n
 \xrightarrow{\quad h\quad}\mathcal W_{n-2}
 \xrightarrow{\quad h\wedge\quad}\mathcal T_{n-4}
 \longrightarrow0,$$ with its arrows scaled by $p$ and $p+1$, respectively, up to nonzero units. The degree-two term has zero outgoing.*


*For a scalar lift at $w_0=9t$, $3\mid n$, the only possible additional coefficient is $$\label{g:V-form}
 V(t,i-2n/3)=t\nu+(i-2n/3)\nu'$$ into a one-dimensional restriction kernel in the plane. The ratio of $\nu,\nu'$ is Frobenius-fixed whenever defined.

On every residue-field entry of Table <a href="#g:finite-table" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="g:finite-table">1</a>, including both subquotients of $\mathsf A$, a normalizing symmetry acts by $$\label{g:character-formula}
 d_0^{\,2m+3n+\phi},\qquad d_0^2=-1,$$ up to the same orientation-twist factor at fixed $i$, where $$\phi=0\quad\text{on scalar labels and }\mathsf x,\mathsf X,
 \qquad
 \phi=2\quad\text{on all other labels}.$$ Consequently a differential of length $R$ can join a source and target only if $$\label{g:character-test}
 R-\Delta j+3\Delta n+\phi_{\mathrm{target}}
 -\phi_{\mathrm{source}}\equiv0\pmod4.$$

The entries $\mathsf a_0,\mathsf a_2$ and their paired $\mathsf R_0,\mathsf R_2$ are removed at length forty-nine. Afterwards $\mathsf a,\mathsf R$ have a common survivor flag only at residue one.*


*Let $C_t$ denote the common survivor flag of $\mathsf c_t$ and $\mathsf M_{t+1}$, paired at length thirty-three. Then $$\label{g:Q-C}
 Q_0=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad C_1=0.$$

The base $\mathcal B$ has cohomological dimension at most two for 3-local local coefficient systems, and $$\widetilde H^*(\mathcal B;A)=0$$ for every constant 3-local abelian group $A$. Moreover, $$\label{cube:thom-identifications}
 D^\circ(S^1)\simeq\Sigma^4M,\qquad
 D^\circ(S^n)\simeq\Sigma^NM.$$ These identifications can be chosen so that $$\label{cube:quotient-cube}
 D^\circ(qa_0)=x^3 1_M:
       \Sigma^NM\longrightarrow\Sigma^4M.$$

There is a triangle $$\label{cube:T-triangle}
 \Sigma^3M\xrightarrow{g}M\xrightarrow{j_T}T
      \xrightarrow{\pi_T}\Sigma^4M$$ and a map $F:\Sigma^NM\to T$ such that $$\pi_TF=x^3 1_M.$$ Both $j_T$ and the natural map $D^\circ M\to T$ are isomorphisms on bottom mod-three homology.

For $k\geq1$, multiplication by $b^k$ into $D_{f,d}$ is surjective if $$\label{finite:surjective-bound}
 d<S_k(f):=\frac92f+\frac12-6k,$$ and injective if $$\label{finite:injective-bound}
 d<I_k(f):=\frac92f-4-6k=S_k(f-1).$$
